---
import ContentLayout from"../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import ArticleImage from"../../components/ui/ArticleImage.astro";

const path ="/calorie-basics/what-is-a-calorie/";
const description ="What is a calorie? Learn about calorie definition, energy in food, how calories work in your body, and why they matter for weight management.";
const faqs = [
  { q:"What is a calorie?", a:"A calorie is a unit of energy. In nutrition, it measures the energy your body gets from food and drinks. Your body uses this energy for everything you do." },
  { q:"How many calories do you need daily?", a:"Most adults need 1,600-2,800 calories daily depending on sex, age, weight, and activity level. Your personal calorie needs are calculated using TDEE." },
  { q:"Where are calories found in food?", a:"Calories come from macronutrients: protein (4 cal/g), carbohydrates (4 cal/g), fat (9 cal/g), and alcohol (7 cal/g). Our food search shows calories in thousands of foods." },
  { q:"Do all calories equal the same?", a:"While all calories provide energy, the source matters for hormones, satiety, and nutrition. Focus on nutrient-dense foods for sustainable results." },
  { q:"How do you measure calories in food?", a:"Look up nutrition facts online. Track portions and add them up to match your daily target." },
  { q:"What happens when you eat too many calories?", a:"Extra calories are stored as fat. Over time, this leads to weight gain. Reduce intake by 500 calories daily to lose about 1 pound per week." },
  { q:"Why do calories matter for weight loss?", a:"Weight loss requires burning more calories than you eat. Use our <a href='/calorie-deficit-calculator/'>calorie deficit calculator</a> to find your target." },
  { q:"Can you survive without calories?", a:"No. Your body needs calories for energy. Without food, your body burns stored glycogen, then fat and muscle. Starvation is dangerous and unsustainable." },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  title="What is a Calorie? The Ultimate Guide to Energy and Weight Management"
  h1="What is a Calorie?"
  description={description}
  path={path}
  category="Basic Guides"
  categoryPath="/guides/"
  lead="Understand what calories are: the units of energy that power your body, fuel your metabolism, and determine changes in your weight. A science-based deep dive to master your nutrition."
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <div class="not-prose w-full">
    
    <!-- Introduction -->
    <section class="mb-16 mt-8">
      <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-surface/80 to-canvas-soft/80 backdrop-blur-md p-8 sm:p-10 rounded-[2rem] shadow-lg border border-hairline/50 relative overflow-hidden">
        <div class="absolute top-0 right-0 -mt-10 -mr-10 w-40 h-40 bg-blue-400 opacity-10 rounded-full blur-3xl"></div>
        <p class="text-xl sm:text-2xl text-ink leading-relaxed font-medium mb-6 relative z-10">
          The word"calorie" is ubiquitous in our modern culture: we see it on supermarket labels, track it in mobile apps, and discuss it in endless debates about diets and health. But, <strong>what exactly is a calorie?</strong>
        </p>
        <p class="text-lg text-body leading-relaxed relative z-10">
          In its purest and most basic form, a calorie is nothing more than a <strong>unit of energy measurement</strong>. It's not a villain to be feared, nor a physical molecule that maliciously accumulates on your belly. Calories are, quite literally, the fuel that allows your heart to beat, your lungs to expand, and your brain to solve complex problems. Understanding how your body processes this fuel is the absolute foundation for any physical transformation goal.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 1 -->
    <section class="mb-16 space-y-8">
      <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink">1. The Science Behind Calories: Thermodynamics and Energy</h2>
      
      <div class="bg-surface/90 backdrop-blur-md p-8 sm:p-10 rounded-[2rem] shadow-xl border border-hairline">
        <p class="text-lg text-body leading-relaxed mb-6">
          To truly understand calories, we must turn to physics. From a strict scientific perspective, a <strong>calorie (cal)</strong> with a lowercase"c" is defined as the amount of thermal energy needed to raise the temperature of 1 gram of liquid water by exactly 1 degree Celsius, at standard atmospheric pressure.
        </p>
        <p class="text-lg text-body leading-relaxed mb-8">
          However, this unit is tiny. Therefore, in nutrition and health sciences, we use <strong>kilocalories (kcal)</strong>. One kilocalorie equals 1,000 small calories (the energy needed to heat 1 kilogram of water by 1 degree Celsius). In everyday use, the food industry and the general public have adopted the word"Calorie" (often capitalized) to actually refer to kilocalories. When you read"200 Calories" on a chocolate bar, scientifically speaking, you are ingesting 200,000 small calories.
        </p>
        
        <div class="my-10 flex justify-center p-6 bg-canvas-soft rounded-3xl border border-hairline">
          <ArticleImage src="/assets/images/articles/calorie-energy.svg" alt="Scientific illustration showing a calorie as a unit of energy." />
        </div>

        <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-brand-hover mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg class="w-6 h-6" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M13 10V3L4 14h7v7l9-11h-7z"></path></svg>
          The First Law of Thermodynamics
        </h3>
        <p class="text-lg text-body leading-relaxed mb-6">
          The concept of calories applied to the human diet rests directly on the First Law of Thermodynamics (the law of conservation of energy). This law states that energy cannot be created out of nothing nor can it be completely destroyed; it can only change form or be transferred.
        </p>
        <p class="text-lg text-body leading-relaxed">
          Your body acts like an extremely sophisticated combustion engine. The food you consume contains <em>potential chemical energy</em>. Through the complex processes of digestion and cellular metabolism, your body extracts this chemical energy and converts it into <em>kinetic or mechanical energy</em> (to contract your muscles), <em>electrical energy</em> (to transmit nerve impulses), and <em>thermal energy</em> (to maintain your body temperature at 98.6°F / 37°C). Any ingested energy that exceeds the body's current requirements must be conserved according to the laws of physics, being stored primarily in the form of triglycerides (adipose tissue or body fat) for future use.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 2 -->
    <section class="mb-16 space-y-8">
      <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink">2. The Origin of Energy: The 4 Macronutrients</h2>
      
      <p class="text-lg text-body leading-relaxed">
        All measurable energy that enters our organism comes from massive chemical components present in food, known as macronutrients. Each has a specific energy density, which dictates how much energy it provides per gram ingested.
      </p>

      <div class="grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        <!-- Proteins -->
        <div class="bg-surface/80 backdrop-blur-md p-8 rounded-[2rem] shadow-lg border border-hairline hover:shadow-2xl transition-all duration-300 group">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-5">
            <div class="bg-emerald-100 text-emerald-700 p-3 rounded-2xl font-black text-xl tracking-tight group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform">4 kcal/g</div>
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">Proteins</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-body mb-4 text-base leading-relaxed">
            Although they provide the same base energy as carbohydrates, proteins are the fundamental building blocks of the body. Their primary role is not to provide energy, but to build, repair, and maintain tissues, enzymes, hormones, and the immune system. In addition, they have the highest thermic effect (requiring more energy to digest) and provide the greatest satiety of all nutrients.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-hairline">
            <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-mute uppercase tracking-wider">Main Sources:</span>
            <p class="text-sm text-body mt-1">Chicken breast, fish, eggs, Greek yogurt, legumes, tofu, seitan.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        
        <!-- Carbohydrates -->
        <div class="bg-surface/80 backdrop-blur-md p-8 rounded-[2rem] shadow-lg border border-hairline hover:shadow-2xl transition-all duration-300 group">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-5">
            <div class="bg-brand-soft text-brand-hover p-3 rounded-2xl font-black text-xl tracking-tight group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform">4 kcal/g</div>
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">Carbohydrates</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-body mb-4 text-base leading-relaxed">
            They are the body's preferred source of energy, especially for the brain and during high-intensity physical activities (such as weightlifting or sprinting). The body breaks them down into glucose. Short-term excess is stored as glycogen in the liver and muscles; long-term excess is converted into fat.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-hairline">
            <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-mute uppercase tracking-wider">Main Sources:</span>
            <p class="text-sm text-body mt-1">Oats, rice, whole wheat bread, pasta, potatoes, fruits, vegetables, and refined sugars.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Fats -->
        <div class="bg-surface/80 backdrop-blur-md p-8 rounded-[2rem] shadow-lg border border-hairline hover:shadow-2xl transition-all duration-300 group">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-5">
            <div class="bg-amber-100 text-amber-700 p-3 rounded-2xl font-black text-xl tracking-tight group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform">9 kcal/g</div>
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">Fats (Lipids)</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-body mb-4 text-base leading-relaxed">
            Fats are the most calorically dense macronutrient (more than double that of proteins and carbohydrates). They are crucial for the absorption of fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K), the synthesis of steroid hormones (such as testosterone), and the protection of organs. Although they have been unfairly demonized, their high density requires portion control.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-hairline">
            <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-mute uppercase tracking-wider">Main Sources:</span>
            <p class="text-sm text-body mt-1">Extra virgin olive oil, avocados, nuts, seeds, salmon, egg yolks, whole dairy products.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Alcohol -->
        <div class="bg-surface/80 backdrop-blur-md p-8 rounded-[2rem] shadow-lg border border-hairline hover:shadow-2xl transition-all duration-300 group">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mb-5">
            <div class="bg-purple-100 text-purple-700 p-3 rounded-2xl font-black text-xl tracking-tight group-hover:scale-110 transition-transform">7 kcal/g</div>
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink">Alcohol (Ethanol)</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-body mb-4 text-base leading-relaxed">
            Although it is not an essential nutrient, alcohol provides a massive amount of energy. It is known as a provider of"empty calories" because it offers no nutritional value, vitamins, or minerals. Even worse, the body identifies it as a toxin, so it stops fat burning and prioritizes its metabolism, storing any other nutrient ingested simultaneously as body fat.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-4 pt-4 border-t border-hairline">
            <span class="text-sm font-semibold text-mute uppercase tracking-wider">Main Sources:</span>
            <p class="text-sm text-body mt-1">Wine, beer, distilled spirits, cocktails (which also add sugars).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      
      <!-- Expert Tip -->
      <div class="relative overflow-hidden bg-gradient-to-r from-amber-50 to-orange-50 border border-amber-200 p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-3xl mt-10 shadow-sm">
        <div class="absolute top-0 right-0 p-4 opacity-10">
          <svg class="w-24 h-24 text-amber-500" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 20 20"><path fill-rule="evenodd" d="M18 10a8 8 0 11-16 0 8 8 0 0116 0zm-7-4a1 1 0 11-2 0 1 1 0 012 0zM9 9a1 1 0 000 2v3a1 1 0 001 1h1a1 1 0 100-2v-3a1 1 0 00-1-1H9z" clip-rule="evenodd"></path></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="relative z-10 flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-6 items-center">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 bg-surface p-4 rounded-full shadow-md text-amber-500">
            <svg class="w-8 h-8" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9.663 17h4.673M12 3v1m6.364 1.636l-.707.707M21 12h-1M4 12H3m3.343-5.657l-.707-.707m2.828 9.9a5 5 0 117.072 0l-.548.547A3.374 3.374 0 0014 18.469V19a2 2 0 11-4 0v-.531c0-.895-.356-1.754-.988-2.386l-.548-.547z"></path></svg>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h4 class="text-xl font-bold text-amber-900 mb-2">Expert Tip: The Secret of Water and Fiber</h4>
            <p class="text-amber-800 text-lg">
              Water and most types of dietary fiber <strong>do not provide absorbable calories</strong>. Consuming foods rich in fiber and water, such as cruciferous vegetables, leafy greens, and fresh fruits, adds massive volume to your meals, distends the stomach walls, and triggers satiety receptors, <em>without adding extra energy to your diet</em>. This principle, known as"low calorie density," is the most powerful tool for losing fat without going hungry.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 3 -->
    <section class="mb-16 space-y-8">
      <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink">3. Caloric Expenditure: How Does Your Body Burn Energy?</h2>
      
      <p class="text-lg text-body leading-relaxed">
        The concept of"burning calories" is often misunderstood. Your <strong>Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)</strong> is the sum of all the energy your body uses in a 24-hour period. For the vast majority of people, running on the treadmill or lifting weights represents a tiny fraction of total calories burned. Metabolism is divided into four main pillars:
      </p>

      <div class="relative mt-12 bg-surface rounded-[2rem] shadow-xl border border-hairline overflow-hidden">
        <div class="absolute top-0 left-0 w-2 h-full bg-gradient-to-b from-indigo-500 via-purple-500 to-pink-500"></div>
        <div class="p-8 sm:p-12 space-y-10">
          
          <!-- BMR -->
          <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-6 md:gap-8 items-start">
            <div class="w-full md:w-40 flex-shrink-0">
              <div class="bg-brand-soft text-brand-hover border border-hairline py-3 px-4 rounded-xl text-center font-black text-3xl shadow-sm">
                60-75%
              </div>
              <div class="text-center mt-2 text-sm font-bold text-mute uppercase">of daily total</div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
                Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR)
              </h3>
              <p class="text-body leading-relaxed text-lg">
                This is the massive energy needed to keep you alive at complete rest. Imagine spending 24 hours in a coma or asleep in bed; your body still requires an enormous amount of energy to pump blood, inflate lungs, synthesize new cells, filter blood in the kidneys, and maintain neuronal function and a temperature of 98.6 degrees. BMR is strongly dictated by the amount of lean muscle mass you possess (muscle is metabolically expensive to maintain).
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- NEAT -->
          <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-6 md:gap-8 items-start">
            <div class="w-full md:w-40 flex-shrink-0">
              <div class="bg-purple-50 text-purple-700 border border-purple-200 py-3 px-4 rounded-xl text-center font-black text-3xl shadow-sm">
                15-20%
              </div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Non-Exercise Activity Thermogenesis (NEAT)</h3>
              <p class="text-body leading-relaxed text-lg">
                NEAT encompasses all the energy burned by subconscious, spontaneous, and routine physical activities that do not qualify as"sports." Walking to the kitchen, standing while working at your desk, gesturing while talking, maintaining posture, cleaning the house, or nervously tapping your foot (fidgeting). NEAT can vary drastically from person to person; a construction worker will burn thousands more calories in NEAT than a sedentary office worker.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- TEF -->
          <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-6 md:gap-8 items-start">
            <div class="w-full md:w-40 flex-shrink-0">
              <div class="bg-pink-50 text-pink-700 border border-pink-200 py-3 px-4 rounded-xl text-center font-black text-3xl shadow-sm">
                10%
              </div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Thermic Effect of Food (TEF)</h3>
              <p class="text-body leading-relaxed text-lg">
                You burn calories simply by digesting the food you eat! The body spends energy chewing, generating stomach acid, absorbing nutrients in the intestines, and transporting them through the bloodstream. Fats have a low TEF (0-3%), carbohydrates moderate (5-10%), but proteins have an exceptionally high TEF (20-30%). This means that for every 100 pure calories of chicken breast, your body spends 30 just to process it, leaving a net gain of 70.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <!-- EAT -->
          <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-6 md:gap-8 items-start">
            <div class="w-full md:w-40 flex-shrink-0">
              <div class="bg-rose-50 text-rose-700 border border-rose-200 py-3 px-4 rounded-xl text-center font-black text-3xl shadow-sm">
                5-10%
              </div>
            </div>
            <div>
              <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Exercise Activity Thermogenesis (EAT)</h3>
              <p class="text-body leading-relaxed text-lg">
                This is the energy spent during structured and intentional exercise: running, weightlifting, swimming, taking CrossFit classes, etc. Despite strenuous efforts, for the average person, this represents the smallest piece of the daily caloric pie. That is why the saying"you can't out-train a bad diet" is metabolically true.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>

        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 4 -->
    <section class="mb-16 space-y-8">
      <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink">4. Energy Balance: The Unbreakable Law of Weight</h2>
      
      <p class="text-lg text-body leading-relaxed">
        When you strip away the passing fads, fitness gurus, and deceptive marketing, human weight management is governed by an irrefutable thermodynamic principle: <strong>energy balance</strong>. It is the direct relationship between energy in (food and drink) and energy out (TDEE).
      </p>

      <div class="grid md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 mt-10">
        <!-- Surplus -->
        <div class="bg-surface p-8 rounded-3xl border-2 border-rose-100 shadow-lg text-center flex flex-col items-center hover:border-rose-300 transition-colors">
          <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-rose-400 to-red-500 text-white w-20 h-20 rounded-2xl flex items-center justify-center font-black text-4xl mb-6 shadow-md shadow-rose-200 transform rotate-3">+</div>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Caloric Surplus</h3>
          <p class="text-rose-600 mb-5 text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest bg-rose-50 px-3 py-1 rounded-full">Calories In > Calories Out</p>
          <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed">You consume more energy than your body demands. The surplus is stored as adipose tissue (fat) or, if you apply a severe hypertrophy stimulus and consume enough protein, as lean muscle. <strong>Final result: Body weight gain.</strong></p>
        </div>
        
        <!-- Maintenance -->
        <div class="bg-surface p-8 rounded-3xl border-2 border-hairline shadow-lg text-center flex flex-col items-center hover:border-hairline transition-colors">
          <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-slate-500 to-gray-700 text-white w-20 h-20 rounded-2xl flex items-center justify-center font-black text-4xl mb-6 shadow-md shadow-slate-200 transform -rotate-3">=</div>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Maintenance Level</h3>
          <p class="text-body mb-5 text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest bg-canvas-soft-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full">Calories In = Calories Out</p>
          <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed">Your intake matches your expenditure perfectly. The body uses all provided energy with no need to store surpluses or cannibalize its own reserves. <strong>Final result: Total weight stability.</strong></p>
        </div>

        <!-- Deficit -->
        <div class="bg-surface p-8 rounded-3xl border-2 border-emerald-100 shadow-lg text-center flex flex-col items-center hover:border-emerald-300 transition-colors">
          <div class="bg-gradient-to-br from-emerald-400 to-green-500 text-white w-20 h-20 rounded-2xl flex items-center justify-center font-black text-4xl mb-6 shadow-md shadow-emerald-200 transform rotate-3">-</div>
          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Caloric Deficit</h3>
          <p class="text-emerald-700 mb-5 text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest bg-emerald-50 px-3 py-1 rounded-full">Calories In < Calories Out</p>
          <p class="text-body text-base leading-relaxed">You do not provide enough external energy through food to cover daily demands. The body is forced to mobilize and oxidize its internal reserves (stored fat) to make up the difference. <strong>Final result: Guaranteed weight loss.</strong></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 5 -->
    <section class="mb-16 space-y-8">
      <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink">5. Are All Calories Created Equal?</h2>
      
      <div class="bg-surface/80 backdrop-blur-md p-8 sm:p-12 rounded-[2rem] shadow-xl border border-hairline">
        <p class="text-lg text-body leading-relaxed mb-8">
          There is a fierce debate between those who claim that"a calorie is a calorie" and those who assert that the type of food is all that matters. Scientific reality embraces both extremes depending on the context:
        </p>

        <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-10">
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-brand-hover mb-4 border-b-2 border-hairline pb-2">For Weight Loss (Pure Physics)</h3>
            <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-6">
              <strong>Yes, all calories are exactly equal.</strong> If you establish a strict caloric deficit by eating exclusively ice cream and cookies, you will lose weight. Dr. Mark Haub, a human nutrition professor at Kansas State University, proved this with his infamous"Twinkie Diet," losing 27 pounds in 10 weeks eating processed cakes, but maintaining a mathematical caloric deficit. The scale responds exclusively to thermodynamics.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-emerald-700 mb-4 border-b-2 border-emerald-100 pb-2">For Health and Body Composition</h3>
            <p class="text-body leading-relaxed mb-6">
              <strong>No, calories are not at all equal.</strong> This is where the body's endocrine (hormonal) response comes into play. The source of calories dictates whether you feel famished or satiated, whether your body burns fat or muscle, and whether your internal inflammation goes up or down. 500 calories of wild salmon and broccoli will provoke a stable insulin response, massive satiety, and provide neuroprotective micronutrients. 500 calories of high-fructose corn syrup will cause dramatic insulin spikes, cellular resistance, acute inflammation, and an insatiable hunger rebound an hour later.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Section 6 -->
    <section class="mb-16 space-y-8">
      <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-ink">6. Step-by-Step Guide: How to Calculate Your Daily Calories</h2>
      
      <p class="text-lg text-body leading-relaxed">
        The most common mistake in weight loss is guessing portions or following generic"1200 calorie" diets printed in magazines. Metabolism is highly individual. Here is how professionals and scientists calculate requirements:
      </p>

      <div class="mt-12 space-y-8">
        
        <!-- Step 1 -->
        <div class="bg-surface rounded-3xl p-6 sm:p-8 flex flex-col md:flex-row items-center md:items-start gap-6 border border-hairline shadow-md">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-16 h-16 rounded-2xl bg-brand text-white flex items-center justify-center font-black text-3xl shadow-lg shadow-indigo-200">
            1
          </div>
          <div class="flex-1 text-center md:text-left">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Find Your Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR)</h3>
            <p class="text-body leading-relaxed text-lg mb-4">
              The most widely accepted and accurate scientific formula today is the Mifflin-St Jeor equation. It requires entering your weight, height, age, and sex. For example, a 30-year-old man, weighing 176 lbs (80 kg) and 5'11" (180 cm) tall, has a base BMR of approximately 1,825 calories per day.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 2 -->
        <div class="bg-surface rounded-3xl p-6 sm:p-8 flex flex-col md:flex-row items-center md:items-start gap-6 border border-hairline shadow-md">
          <div class="flex-shrink-0 w-16 h-16 rounded-2xl bg-brand text-white flex items-center justify-center font-black text-3xl shadow-lg shadow-indigo-200">
            2
          </div>
          <div class="flex-1 text-center md:text-left">
            <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-3">Apply the Activity Multiplier (PAL) to Get Your TDEE</h3>
            <p class="text-body leading-relaxed text-lg mb-4">
              You must multiply your BMR by a Physical Activity Level (PAL) factor. If you have a desk job and don't exercise, you multiply by 1.2. If you do light exercise 1-3 times a week, by 1.375. If you train hard 3-5 days, by 1.55. If you are an athlete or construction worker, by 1.725 or more.
            </p>
            <div class="bg-brand-soft border border-hairline rounded-xl p-4 text-ink text-sm">
              <strong>Example:</strong> 1,825 (BMR) x 1.375 (Light Exercise) = <strong>2,509 Calories/day (TDEE).</strong> This is your maintenance point.
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Step 3 -->
        <div class="bg-surface rounded-3xl p-6 sm:p-8 flex flex-col md:flex-row items-center md:items-start gap-6 border border-hairline shadow-md">
