      <div class="space-y-4">
        <!-- FAQ 1 -->
        <div class="bg-surface/80 backdrop-blur-md p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl shadow-sm border border-hairline">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mb-3 flex items-start">
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-indigo-500 mr-2 shrink-0 mt-0.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M8.228 9c.549-1.165 2.03-2 3.772-2 2.21 0 4 1.343 4 3 0 1.4-1.278 2.575-3.006 2.907-.542.104-.994.54-.994 1.093m0 3h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>
            Is it true that eating late at night makes you fatter because your metabolism slows down?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">
            <strong>False.</strong> Thermodynamics doesn't own a clock. If your daily limit is 2,000 calories, it doesn't matter if you eat your calories at 9 in the morning or at 11 at night; your body will process the total energy balance of the day the exact same way. The myth arises because people who eat late at night often consume ultra-processed foods in front of the TV, unwittingly exceeding their total caloric limit.
          </p>
        </div>
        
        <!-- FAQ 2 -->
        <div class="bg-surface/80 backdrop-blur-md p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl shadow-sm border border-hairline">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mb-3 flex items-start">
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-indigo-500 mr-2 shrink-0 mt-0.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M8.228 9c.549-1.165 2.03-2 3.772-2 2.21 0 4 1.343 4 3 0 1.4-1.278 2.575-3.006 2.907-.542.104-.994.54-.994 1.093m0 3h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>
            What exactly are"negative calories"?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">
            <strong>Negative calories are a myth of diet culture.</strong> It is wrongly claimed that chewing and digesting highly fibrous foods (like celery or lettuce) requires more energy than the food contains. Although the Thermic Effect (TEF) of raw vegetables is high, no natural food requires more energy than it provides. Celery provides about 14 calories and you burn about 2 digesting it; the net balance is still positive (+12 calories). However, they are excellent allies due to their incredible satiety and low net energy yield.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ 3 -->
        <div class="bg-surface/80 backdrop-blur-md p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-2xl shadow-sm border border-hairline">
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-ink mb-3 flex items-start">
            <svg class="w-6 h-6 text-indigo-500 mr-2 shrink-0 mt-0.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M8.228 9c.549-1.165 2.03-2 3.772-2 2.21 0 4 1.343 4 3 0 1.4-1.278 2.575-3.006 2.907-.542.104-.994.54-.994 1.093m0 3h.01M21 12a9 9 0 11-18 0 9 9 0 0118 0z"></path></svg>
            I've drastically lowered my calories but I'm no longer losing weight. Have I"damaged" my metabolism?
          </h3>
          <p class="text-body leading-relaxed">
            <strong>"Metabolic damage" does not exist the way gurus paint it, but"Metabolic Adaptation" does.</strong> When you lose weight and apply a very aggressive deficit, your body, in an evolutionary attempt to avoid starvation, subtly slows down your BMR (you are a smaller organism now, you need less energy), and most importantly, <em>it destroys your NEAT</em>. Without realizing it, you start moving less, blinking less, becoming more lethargic, burning hundreds fewer calories a day. Your metabolism isn't"broken," it has simply adapted to run on less gas.
          </p>
